 ## Background
-Texture-Enhanced Direct Volume Rendering was introduced by Manke and Wünsche in 2009 as an extension to their previous modular GPU-based DVR framework (Manke and Wünsche, 2008) [S1, unofficial]. The research was motivated by the limited capabilities of color and opacity to convey multiple variables and attributes of volumetric data sets [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on previous applications of textures for DVR and is consistent with the use of textures for polygon rendering [S1, unofficial]. It contrasts with Patel et al.'s approach (Patel et al., 2007) which uses 2D textures mapped onto cutting planes [S1, unofficial].
+Texture-Enhanced Direct Volume Rendering was introduced by Manke and Wünsche in 2009 as an extension to their previous modular GPU-based DVR framework (Manke and Wünsche, 2008) [S1, unofficial]. The research was motivated by the limited capabilities of color and opacity to convey multiple variables and attributes of volumetric data sets [S1, unofficial]. The method is consistent with the use of textures for polygon rendering and previous applications of textures for DVR [S1, unofficial]. It contrasts with Patel et al.'s approach (Patel et al., 2007) which uses 2D textures mapped onto cutting planes [S1, unofficial].
 
